WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:06.350
TINO TERESHKO: My
name is Tino Tereshko.

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:10.480
I come from Seattle,
which some people have

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.980
described in the past as the
Amsterdam of the United States.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:16.390
Kind of, I guess.

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:19.930
So I want to, first of all,
thank everyone for being here,

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:23.680
and investing time in coming
here, and listening to us,

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:28.270
and having conversations
with us, because I

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:31.390
heard earlier today that people
in Amsterdam have no time.

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:36.150
So it's especially important for
us to recognize the investments

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:39.670
that you guys have made to come
here and spend time with us.

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:44.050
So real quick, before
we get started,

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:47.630
let's take a selfie, especially
the people here in the front.

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:51.580
On three, I want you to
raise your hands, to smile,

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:52.940
to clap, whatever you want.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.400
Just make it interesting, OK?

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.160
And I'll put it
on Twitter later.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:58.180
So let's do it.

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:01.285
1, 2, 3!

00:01:01.285 --> 00:01:03.145
AUDIENCE: [SHOUTING] Hey!

00:01:03.145 --> 00:01:04.629
TINO TERESHKO: That's good.

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:05.500
That's good.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:06.610
Thank you.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:10.190
So I'm here to talk
about analytics

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:14.320
and the various
spectrums of analytics

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:17.230
that are possible today with
the advancements in technology

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:19.340
by Google and by others.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:21.130
But let's start with
a quick little story.

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:23.680
You probably have
seen these little cars

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:26.810
driving around with a 360
camera on top of them that

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:29.430
say Google Maps on top of them.

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.420
For the past 10
years or so, Google

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:34.870
has been mapping the entire
world, or as much as possible,

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:37.030
and externalizing that
information through Google

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.010
Maps, through Google Earth.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:41.410
You're able to take a
little yellow stick figure

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:43.510
and put it anywhere
in the world and walk

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:44.940
through the neighborhoods.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:46.900
I do that with
neighborhoods that I grew up

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:48.606
in that I haven't
been there in a while.

00:01:48.606 --> 00:01:49.980
But the interesting
thing is this

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:54.850
is, of course, a massive data
collection and optimization

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:55.780
problem.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:00.490
And it really speaks to how
heavily invested in data

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:02.080
Google is itself.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.840
But the interesting thing
here is, up until recently,

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:09.259
up until the last four or five
years, this was just imagery,

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:09.759
right?

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:11.897
You couldn't-- and then maybe
coordinates and things like

00:02:11.897 --> 00:02:12.610
that.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:15.960
You couldn't really gain as much
insight as possible from this.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.420
But with advancements in
AI and with advancements

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:22.690
in commoditization of computing
hardware and improvements

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:25.980
in computing power, especially
in AI machine learning,

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.350
we're able to derive further
information from this imagery.

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:32.402
So, for example, we can
understand that the business

00:02:32.402 --> 00:02:33.360
has turned over, right?

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:34.550
There's a new business
in place there.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:36.280
So we can go into
Google Maps and update

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:37.600
the place of business.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.580
Or maybe this sign was
wasn't there before.

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:42.121
Maybe it was a two-way street,
but now you can't go this way.

00:02:42.121 --> 00:02:44.350
So it's very important
for us to tell you

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:46.610
that you can't go
down this road.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:48.610
So all of this, of course,
is done automatically

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:51.520
through our AI
learning capabilities.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.570
And so this right here is
probably the worst kept secret

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:57.400
in Silicon Valley.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.890
Google is very serious
about analytics.

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:01.000
It says so in our model.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:07.276
It says, Google's
mission is to--

00:03:07.276 --> 00:03:10.960
gosh-- organize all the data
that's possible in the world

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:13.670
and present it for bad or
good, for everyone, right?

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:16.660
And so Google has built
a number of technologies

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:21.150
that it uses internally to
organize this information.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:24.270
And the premise of
Google Cloud Platform

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.540
is to be able to externalize
these technologies,

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:30.780
and the lessons learned,
and the power of compute

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:31.900
for your benefits.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:35.980
So you can run alongside Google,
leveraging the same Google

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:38.800
investments and
technology and innovation

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:41.860
for yourselves as well,
which hopefully accelerates

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:45.340
your pace of innovation,
accelerates your iteration,

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:48.580
allows you to deliver faster,
better, more interesting

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:53.320
features to a lot
of your customers.

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:56.770
And the fact that Google has
been a data-driven company

00:03:56.770 --> 00:04:00.460
for such a long time and has
built these services that

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:04.330
allow you to do some really
powerful things with data

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:07.634
analytics has made
it fairly easy

00:04:07.634 --> 00:04:09.550
for us to become a
machine-learning company as

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:10.220
well.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:11.840
This chart probably
needs updating.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:14.620
But essentially, for the
past five or so years,

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:18.190
virtually every single product
and service that Google has has

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:21.519
become, in some ways--
and often, in many ways--

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:22.970
AI-driven, right?

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:26.860
So if you use Google Photos,
for example you can very clearly

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:31.870
understand the type of inference
that occurs on your content

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:33.250
automatically, right?

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:36.340
It allows you to
create stories based

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:37.900
on just the imagery
that's happened

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:41.799
and the ability to pull out
metadata about that imagery.

00:04:41.799 --> 00:04:44.340
But, of course, you can have
too much of a good thing, right?

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:47.080
Going back to that example
where Google has generated

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:50.010
a whole lot of photos of
a whole lot of streets

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.660
around the world, and if
you need to go back in time

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:54.460
and process all
that information,

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.580
that is a very serious
computing power.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.580
So you can have too much
of a good thing, right?

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:00.430
You can have too
much of a requirement

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:02.680
for AI, especially
for inference,

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:07.830
which is asking the model what
the model thinks is happening.

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:09.540
So in the last few
years, Google has

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:12.960
been faced with a problem,
a serious problem, right?

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:16.380
We have too much
demand for inference.

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:20.910
And we we're faced with
having to expand into dozens

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:22.230
and dozens of new data centers.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:24.313
And if you've ever seen a
picture of a data center

00:05:24.313 --> 00:05:26.870
that Google operates,
these are huge things.

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:28.372
They're bigger than
this venue here.

00:05:28.372 --> 00:05:29.580
So it's a serious investment.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:31.790
It takes time.

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:33.990
It's complex.

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:37.860
So we implemented Tensor
Processing Units--

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:40.870
TPUs-- and now,
we're on version 2.0.

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:45.030
TPUs are specially
optimized for matrix algebra

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:52.640
that allows you to essentially
infer results of an AI model.

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:58.150
Essentially, TPUs allow us to
save a whole lot of energy.

00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:00.330
And so these are
live inside of Google

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:01.770
for the past several years.

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:02.970
They're intricate.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:03.930
They're delicate.

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:06.840
And they're really important
to our infrastructure.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:09.090
So on to a little bit
more practical terms.

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:11.640
When I talk to
data scientists, AI

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:17.220
experts, machine-learning
experts, they tend to say,

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:18.870
in day-to-day
activities of what they

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:22.050
do, some folks tell
me, well, I have

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:24.720
to operate an HBase
cluster, because that's

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.490
where our data is kept.

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:31.980
Or I have to take data from
Hadoop, run a Hive query,

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:34.440
create a cohort, and feed
that into a model that

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:36.010
lives somewhere else.

00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:38.070
Or I have to run a
bunch of SQL queries

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:40.200
before I can even feed
anything into my model

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:43.150
to randomize samples,
and so on and so forth.

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:47.170
So ultimately, folks tell me
that 90% of really true AI

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:49.410
machine learning
workloads involve

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:53.190
pure data science or maybe
even data engineering, right?

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:56.970
So when you're faced with
complex problems like that,

00:06:56.970 --> 00:07:00.060
it's really important to
try to minimize complexity

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:03.220
wherever it's not necessary.

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:05.910
So this is really something
interesting to keep in mind.

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:08.950
You can get lost in
the noise very easily.

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:12.630
But then, according to
Gartner, a whole lot

00:07:12.630 --> 00:07:15.720
of projects that try to create
big data infrastructure, Data

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:17.910
Lakes, end up failing.

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:20.490
I'm not sure how accurate
that statement is,

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:22.990
but it's quite possibly true.

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:23.490
Right?

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:27.090
So it's very hard to do
big data in 2017, right?

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:27.920
Hadoop's complex.

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:29.880
HDFS is complex.

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:31.570
You need to have a
lot of expertise.

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:33.660
So how do you compute this?

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:39.390
If you want to be an AI-driven
organization, and [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:42.110
data science, but 90% of
data science projects fail,

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:46.140
that's kind of a daunting
proposition, right?

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:49.849
So I like to use
this Jenga analogy.

00:07:49.849 --> 00:07:51.890
You guys are probably
familiar with Jenga, right?

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:56.785
It's basically a game where
you pile on wooden bricks

00:07:56.785 --> 00:07:57.660
on top of each other.

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:00.580
And you pull out a brick from
here, and you put it on top.

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:03.170
So you want to think
about data infrastructure

00:08:03.170 --> 00:08:04.830
as entirely interdependent.

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:07.380
Bricks on top depend on
bricks on the bottom,

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:09.600
and you want to have
a very clean Jenga

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:11.532
tower so it doesn't topple.

00:08:11.532 --> 00:08:13.490
So at the bottom, you
could think about this as

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:14.700
baseline architecture.

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:17.100
What I mean by that is you
want to have good networking.

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:21.607
You want to choose a good
public cloud provider or two.

00:08:21.607 --> 00:08:23.940
You might want to have a
really good data center, right?

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:26.960
You want to get that right.

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:29.070
And on top of that,
you want to have really

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:32.789
good compute, Virtual
machines, servers, containers,

00:08:32.789 --> 00:08:34.380
and good storage.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:37.620
object storage, for example.

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:41.309
And that aids you in developing
your good applications

00:08:41.309 --> 00:08:44.070
and databases that power
these applications.

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:45.990
So in order to have
good applications,

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:48.780
you probably need to
have those two right.

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:51.979
Only then you can start thinking
about a proper data warehouse.

00:08:51.979 --> 00:08:53.520
You can certainly
do a data warehouse

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:54.811
without getting all that right.

00:08:54.811 --> 00:08:58.550
But it really helps you to have
the foundational blocks stacked

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:00.540
up before data warehouse.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:03.610
You're probably going to
say, Tino, data warehouse

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:05.265
is kind of an old term.

00:09:05.265 --> 00:09:06.370
We don't use that anymore.

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:08.850
We have a data lake.

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:10.200
Sure.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:14.220
But sometimes data
lakes kind of get stale,

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:18.620
and they turn into data swamps.

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:21.810
But you drain the swamp, and
you have everything right.

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:23.580
And now your data
lake is so big,

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:25.650
you can call it
a data ocean now.

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:26.940
Fantastic.

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:29.760
Well, let's just-- you know,
let's just summarize all that,

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:34.920
and just use one umbrella,
data, large body of water.

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:36.435
I coined that.

00:09:36.435 --> 00:09:38.010
You can ask me to use it.

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:41.160
But that's mine, data
large body of water.

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:43.651
And once you have data
large body of water set up,

00:09:43.651 --> 00:09:45.900
then you can start thinking
about AI machine learning.

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:48.121
There are some exceptions
you guys saw earlier today.

00:09:48.121 --> 00:09:49.620
And I can talk about
them some more.

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:53.230
But that's really
the lesson here.

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:55.320
And of course, it's a
Jenga tower analogy,

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:57.730
so if you build a
bad Jenga tower,

00:09:57.730 --> 00:09:59.790
it's probably going
to topple, hence

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:03.250
the 90% of all data
lake projects fail.

00:10:03.250 --> 00:10:05.480
That's the lesson here.

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:09.590
OK, so now that I've
scared you sufficiently,

00:10:09.590 --> 00:10:12.950
let's talk about what a
modern data analytics stack

00:10:12.950 --> 00:10:14.270
looks like.

00:10:14.270 --> 00:10:17.000
There's probably a
whole lot of attributes

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:20.150
that you can identify
that you can say,

00:10:20.150 --> 00:10:23.990
this is what has to be in a
modern data analytics stack.

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:28.170
So I have four that I
want to talk to you about.

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:33.150
The first one is separation
of storage and compute.

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:35.130
And that is a little
bit of a loaded term.

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:38.696
So let me quantify exactly
what's happening here.

00:10:38.696 --> 00:10:40.070
Separation of
storage and compute

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:42.410
is really a solved problem.

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:46.470
It buys you so many options when
you're designing your system.

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:48.920
If you don't have this,
and you are operating

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:51.890
on a decently sized
scale, you should

00:10:51.890 --> 00:10:53.480
look into this yesterday.

00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:55.970
It's just the pros
and cons of having

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:59.360
this attribute are just
tremendous in your favor,

00:10:59.360 --> 00:10:59.930
right?

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:01.490
So this is a solved problem.

00:11:01.490 --> 00:11:02.100
Get it done.

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:04.100
Here's what I mean by
pure separation of storage

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:05.600
and compute.

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:06.690
It's exactly that, right?

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:11.090
There's a fine line between
processing and compute storage.

00:11:11.090 --> 00:11:14.230
And that fine line
actually represents

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:17.960
a very powerful networking,
networking that can talk,

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:20.720
that allows every node
within the data center

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:23.320
to talk to every other node
within the same data center,

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:26.630
at 10 gig, all at
the same time, right?

00:11:26.630 --> 00:11:30.110
So Google's data
centers last reported

00:11:30.110 --> 00:11:33.610
have over a petabit of
bisectional bandwidth

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:35.587
per second per data center.

00:11:35.587 --> 00:11:36.920
So that's kind of a requirement.

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:38.295
You have to have
good networking.

00:11:38.295 --> 00:11:39.890
But ultimately, in
practical terms,

00:11:39.890 --> 00:11:41.810
you can have your
various storage layers,

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:44.560
whether you have
BigTable, which is our--

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:46.410
Google's NoSQL database.

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:49.460
You can have BigQuery storage,
which is our data warehousing

00:11:49.460 --> 00:11:50.660
data lake storage.

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:54.110
Or you can have Object Store,
where you keep your cat videos.

00:11:54.110 --> 00:11:56.420
And you can have
Google Cloud Dataflow,

00:11:56.420 --> 00:11:59.750
for example, which is our
batch and stream processing

00:11:59.750 --> 00:12:02.180
unified programming
model and engine,

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:05.540
access all of those,
regardless of where they live.

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:07.370
Or potentially, you
can have BigQuery SQL,

00:12:07.370 --> 00:12:10.895
which is standard, run SQL
queries on all of those--

00:12:10.895 --> 00:12:14.360
not cat videos, but just
ignore that for a second.

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:16.510
And you can have your
own Hadoop and Spark

00:12:16.510 --> 00:12:19.590
clusters run on top of
that information as well.

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:22.700
So I'm not discounting
the significance

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:26.000
that Hadoop and Spark have
in today's environment.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:28.620
The momentum behind these
projects is phenomenal, right?

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:30.770
So we have Google
Cloud Dataproc,

00:12:30.770 --> 00:12:32.720
which is our fully
managed Spark,

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:35.660
Hadoop and Flink service.

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:37.820
The awesome thing
about our service

00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:41.570
specifically is that you can get
clusters very, very quickly--

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:42.170
90 seconds.

00:12:42.170 --> 00:12:44.390
Hopefully you guys
see that over there.

00:12:44.390 --> 00:12:49.430
90 seconds is 99th
percentile from the moment

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:52.910
you push the button to the
moment your cluster is live,

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:54.280
ready to use.

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:57.290
That's very fast,
especially within--

00:12:57.290 --> 00:13:01.040
you know, if you're coming from
your own on-premise private

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:03.440
data center space,
the typical numbers

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:07.400
that I hear involve six to nine
months, sometimes even longer.

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:09.860
So 90 seconds is just a
phenomenal change, right?

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:11.964
It enables so many options.

00:13:11.964 --> 00:13:13.880
And, of course, we have
pay per minute, right?

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:15.920
So you can shut this thing
down after 20 minutes

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:16.635
if you'd like to.

00:13:16.635 --> 00:13:19.490
You save the results
into storage.

00:13:19.490 --> 00:13:22.040
That's where the state is.

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:25.730
And because we have fully
[? femoral ?] preemptible VMs--

00:13:25.730 --> 00:13:28.940
which, kind of disappear
every once in awhile

00:13:28.940 --> 00:13:32.380
in exchange for 80%
off of list price.

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:35.960
So you can have very
quick Hadoop clusters

00:13:35.960 --> 00:13:37.880
that you can shut down
whenever you want,

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:40.520
because you pay by the
minute, and you actually

00:13:40.520 --> 00:13:42.110
don't pay a whole lot for it.

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:44.300
And so the idea is that
Hadoop doesn't really

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:45.200
keep a lot of state.

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:49.100
Unless it's intermediate state,
state lives in storage, right?

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:54.710
Initial state and output lives
in any of these storage pieces.

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:59.660
The other really big point here
is that data silos are bad.

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:01.490
You're playing the
game of telephone

00:14:01.490 --> 00:14:03.710
with data when
you're moving data

00:14:03.710 --> 00:14:05.360
around needlessly,
ultimately, you'll

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:07.400
end up with multiple
copies of data.

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:08.750
And a funny thing happens.

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:12.020
When two data scientists
try to get the same answers

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:13.850
to the same problem
using the same data,

00:14:13.850 --> 00:14:15.680
if that data lives
in other places,

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:17.600
they'll come up with
different results.

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:19.410
I'm still not sure
how that happens.

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:21.720
But data silos are
bad, amd separation

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:25.440
of storage and compute allows
you not to deal with that.

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:28.910
So here's another different
analogy that I can use.

00:14:28.910 --> 00:14:31.790
Well, first, of all, I
apologize, especially

00:14:31.790 --> 00:14:33.620
to the CXOs.

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:35.450
This is-- you know,
we don't think of you

00:14:35.450 --> 00:14:39.320
that poorly from
these stick figures.

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:42.860
But ultimately, this is what
your potential organization

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:43.720
could look like.

00:14:43.720 --> 00:14:47.120
Even at a startup, you can
have this type of delineation.

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:48.980
And you can have
your various bits

00:14:48.980 --> 00:14:52.090
of data living inside of the
query, all the way from very,

00:14:52.090 --> 00:14:55.190
very raw data that comes
from application databases

00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:58.550
and services, to data that's
potentially sensitive that you

00:14:58.550 --> 00:15:01.940
don't necessarily want to have
floating around everywhere,

00:15:01.940 --> 00:15:05.420
to real-time
dashboards, things that

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:07.940
tell you the state
of your business,

00:15:07.940 --> 00:15:09.940
and all the way to data
that you can potentially

00:15:09.940 --> 00:15:12.640
want to externalize
from your infrastructure

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:14.890
to your third parties,
to clients, because they

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:17.350
find use in that.

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:19.320
So they all have
different roles here.

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:21.410
And the nice thing about
our separation of storage

00:15:21.410 --> 00:15:24.160
and compute, just like with
Google Docs, or Google Sheets,

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:27.400
where you can, in [? Place ?]
share information and share

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:29.740
collateral with other folks
within your organizations

00:15:29.740 --> 00:15:32.820
outside, you can do the same
thing with our data lake--

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:34.660
data body of water--

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:35.500
services.

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:39.725
So IT and Ops could potentially
have the overreaching role

00:15:39.725 --> 00:15:40.600
of this organization.

00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:42.610
They want to own
every single data set.

00:15:42.610 --> 00:15:44.410
They want to administer that.

00:15:44.410 --> 00:15:45.200
That's fine.

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:47.530
And so in our Access
Controls, they say,

00:15:47.530 --> 00:15:50.200
I'm the owner and everything,
just like in Google Docs.

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:52.090
But the data engineers
may only need

00:15:52.090 --> 00:15:55.439
to have your access
to the really raw data

00:15:55.439 --> 00:15:56.980
sets, the really
important data sets,

00:15:56.980 --> 00:15:58.540
because you don't
want to mess that up.

00:15:58.540 --> 00:16:00.690
But then, they're the ones
that are creating dashboards.

00:16:00.690 --> 00:16:02.960
They're the ones that are
creating these data sets.

00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:05.450
And so they have
editor access to that.

00:16:05.450 --> 00:16:07.120
And, of course, the
obvious follows.

00:16:07.120 --> 00:16:07.800
Right?

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:11.170
Your executives get a real-time
dashboard in View mode,

00:16:11.170 --> 00:16:14.230
and you can even
share in [? Place ?]

00:16:14.230 --> 00:16:16.180
without having an FTB server.

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:19.420
In [? Place, ?] you can share
your data with third parties,

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:22.120
so they can run SQL
analytics in real time

00:16:22.120 --> 00:16:25.600
without having to do a whole
lot of work on top of your data.

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:26.886
So it's pretty awesome.

00:16:26.886 --> 00:16:28.690
And I've showed this already.

00:16:28.690 --> 00:16:30.520
Data silos are bad.

00:16:30.520 --> 00:16:33.450
It's a relic of
old architectures--

00:16:33.450 --> 00:16:37.480
tried to avoid that ourselves.

00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:41.640
So the second aspect of modern
analytics I want to talk about

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:43.980
is this concept of serverless.

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:48.750
And of course, you've heard
of the various terms that--

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:54.600
or the various contacts within
which server is mentioned.

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:58.650
So the most confined
definition of serverless is--

00:16:58.650 --> 00:17:01.690
it's actually Cloud Functions,
tiny little pieces of code

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:03.480
that execute one at a time.

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:08.339
But the industry has been
extending this buzzword further

00:17:08.339 --> 00:17:10.260
into identifying
some things that

00:17:10.260 --> 00:17:12.260
have a very high
level of abstraction,

00:17:12.260 --> 00:17:14.069
some things that
are fully managed,

00:17:14.069 --> 00:17:16.690
that essentially allow you
to use a [? femoral, ?]

00:17:16.690 --> 00:17:19.440
very, very scalable
piece of compute,

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:23.130
and only pay for the
resources that you use.

00:17:23.130 --> 00:17:28.800
So our serverless analytics
kind of central piece

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:30.600
here is BigQuery.

00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:33.240
Actually, I was on the
BigQuery team before recently,

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:35.295
and it's an amazing product.

00:17:35.295 --> 00:17:38.730
If you haven't used it, you
should definitely look into it.

00:17:38.730 --> 00:17:42.600
BigQuery is our fully managed
analytics data warehouse.

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:44.580
It does have separation
of storage and compute.

00:17:44.580 --> 00:17:46.500
It has a very, very
powerful storage engine

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:48.780
that's self-optimized,
that self-heals.

00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:51.410
It tries to understand how
you're using the storage

00:17:51.410 --> 00:17:53.340
and burns a whole
lot of CPU and RAM

00:17:53.340 --> 00:17:55.260
to optimize the storage
for your workload.

00:17:55.260 --> 00:17:56.600
It does that on your behalf.

00:17:56.600 --> 00:17:59.340
So it's kind of
opinionated in that regard.

00:17:59.340 --> 00:18:01.560
And of course, that
storage component

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:04.590
allows you to share data within
your organization or outside

00:18:04.590 --> 00:18:06.990
of the organization
in very elegant ways

00:18:06.990 --> 00:18:09.360
without having to
copy that data around.

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:12.600
On the other side, BigQuery
has a compute engine.

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:14.520
It's basically Dremel.

00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:17.130
And Dremel-- you can
go read the paper

00:18:17.130 --> 00:18:19.200
that's published by Google.

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:21.990
Dremel executes SQL
queries in real time,

00:18:21.990 --> 00:18:25.000
very fast analytical workloads.

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:28.140
And BigQuery has an incredibly
high level of abstraction.

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:30.030
You never have to
touch hardware.

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:32.940
You never have to define
storage or compute,

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:34.960
or really, not a lot
of configuration there.

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:36.420
And that's on
purpose, because we

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:39.220
want you to focus on
what's important to you.

00:18:39.220 --> 00:18:41.700
So let's take a look at
what BigQuery looks like,

00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:42.680
if you haven't seen it.

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:47.380
So this is the
BigQuery UI, which

00:18:47.380 --> 00:18:51.660
is one of the many ways you
can interact with BigQuery.

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:55.120
And at first, it doesn't
really look all that exciting.

00:18:55.120 --> 00:18:57.940
It kind of looks aged a
little bit, kind of like Gmail

00:18:57.940 --> 00:18:59.320
from 2004.

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:02.860
But just like Gmail from
2004, when you had what?

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:05.060
A gigabyte inbox right away?

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:08.150
When you were yelling
at your grandma

00:19:08.150 --> 00:19:10.750
when she sent you a 30-megabyte
video because your inbox

00:19:10.750 --> 00:19:13.150
would explode, and now
you get a gigabyte.

00:19:13.150 --> 00:19:13.960
That's amazing.

00:19:13.960 --> 00:19:18.400
And so just like Gmail in 2004
hides a whole lot of complexity

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:20.650
and a whole lot of power
underneath the hood

00:19:20.650 --> 00:19:23.420
and a simple UI, BigQuery
does the exact same thing.

00:19:23.420 --> 00:19:25.010
So let me show you.

00:19:25.010 --> 00:19:27.130
So this is-- right
here, BigQuery petabyte

00:19:27.130 --> 00:19:31.810
is a data set, kind of like
a database that BigQuery has.

00:19:31.810 --> 00:19:33.910
And I'm going to click
on this sales partition

00:19:33.910 --> 00:19:37.290
table here, which is a
table that we made up.

00:19:37.290 --> 00:19:39.010
It's not real data.

00:19:39.010 --> 00:19:40.802
We did it for the
purposes of demonstrating

00:19:40.802 --> 00:19:41.718
the power of BigQuery.

00:19:41.718 --> 00:19:43.540
So there's nothing
super special about it.

00:19:43.540 --> 00:19:47.612
I'm going to look at the
schema here of this table.

00:19:47.612 --> 00:19:49.820
What you see here that's
really, really interesting--

00:19:49.820 --> 00:19:52.960
so it's typical, order
execution, when it happens,

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:53.980
how much.

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:56.670
But here we have actually
nested structures,

00:19:56.670 --> 00:19:59.677
like we have an array
inside of the table.

00:19:59.677 --> 00:20:00.510
It's pretty awesome.

00:20:00.510 --> 00:20:03.470
So you can have an array of
an array, JSON-like structures

00:20:03.470 --> 00:20:05.770
that are very, very
complicated, or very simple.

00:20:05.770 --> 00:20:08.860
But it kind of allows you to
have interesting relationships

00:20:08.860 --> 00:20:10.840
with your data here.

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:11.940
So that's a schema.

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:16.430
If I'm going to click on
what the table looks like,

00:20:16.430 --> 00:20:19.990
you'll see here that
the table size is

00:20:19.990 --> 00:20:21.680
a little bit over a petabyte.

00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:23.660
It's a decently sized table.

00:20:23.660 --> 00:20:29.920
And that looks like
a trillion rows,

00:20:29.920 --> 00:20:32.090
a trillion rows in this table.

00:20:32.090 --> 00:20:34.090
So the most sensible thing
to do with this table

00:20:34.090 --> 00:20:37.180
is to try and query it live.

00:20:37.180 --> 00:20:40.096
So I'm going to--

00:20:40.096 --> 00:20:41.890
I have this saved query here.

00:20:49.635 --> 00:20:52.010
So I just push the big red
button that said, Wrong Query.

00:20:52.010 --> 00:20:52.657
Oh, no!

00:20:52.657 --> 00:20:53.740
I has the cache turned on.

00:20:56.990 --> 00:20:58.880
So we're going to not cheat.

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:00.600
We're not going to have cache.

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:02.990
But if you look at this
query, the only thing

00:21:02.990 --> 00:21:04.790
that I'm doing here
to really cheat

00:21:04.790 --> 00:21:08.570
is that this petabyte table
has three years worth of data.

00:21:08.570 --> 00:21:13.210
We're only doing-- we're only
calling one month of partition.

00:21:13.210 --> 00:21:16.400
But otherwise, it's querying
a whole lot of data.

00:21:16.400 --> 00:21:17.990
And it's got a join.

00:21:17.990 --> 00:21:20.840
It's got a big old
regular expression.

00:21:20.840 --> 00:21:23.210
It's got a window
function, everything

00:21:23.210 --> 00:21:26.010
you can possibly imagine
in a typical query, right?

00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:27.590
Mixed bag-- oh!

00:21:27.590 --> 00:21:29.932
Looks like we've finished
this in, you know,

00:21:29.932 --> 00:21:31.670
16 and 1/2 seconds.

00:21:31.670 --> 00:21:34.340
So this table didn't
have anything special.

00:21:34.340 --> 00:21:35.050
There's no keys.

00:21:35.050 --> 00:21:36.010
There's no indexes.

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:37.880
We didn't do any
optimizations, really,

00:21:37.880 --> 00:21:39.920
outside of what the
BigQuery service itself

00:21:39.920 --> 00:21:43.070
provides in terms of
automation and optimization.

00:21:43.070 --> 00:21:47.330
All we did was load
that data into BigQuery.

00:21:47.330 --> 00:21:50.180
So that's the demo here.

00:21:50.180 --> 00:21:51.980
So let's talk about
what exactly happened.

00:21:55.360 --> 00:21:56.560
So I know--

00:21:56.560 --> 00:22:01.630
I've ran this query before,
so I know how much resource

00:22:01.630 --> 00:22:05.170
it takes to execute this
query in under 20 seconds.

00:22:05.170 --> 00:22:07.330
So it's about 3,000 cores.

00:22:07.330 --> 00:22:10.990
So you could potentially
take that data set,

00:22:10.990 --> 00:22:13.180
and if you have 3,000
cores lying around,

00:22:13.180 --> 00:22:14.680
you'd deploy that,
assuming you have

00:22:14.680 --> 00:22:18.400
similar software to BigQuery,
you can execute that.

00:22:18.400 --> 00:22:21.287
You can run your own demo
of that, if you'd like.

00:22:21.287 --> 00:22:22.870
And so that's really
what we did right

00:22:22.870 --> 00:22:27.040
we rented 3,000
cores from Google

00:22:27.040 --> 00:22:29.980
for the length of the job.

00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:32.800
So let's think about this
in virtual machine terms.

00:22:32.800 --> 00:22:35.020
Virtual machines take
minutes to start.

00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:39.610
You get per hour billing,
maybe per minute, maybe.

00:22:39.610 --> 00:22:43.510
And virtual machines-- it's
hard to get 3,000 cores very

00:22:43.510 --> 00:22:44.020
quickly.

00:22:44.020 --> 00:22:44.530
Right?

00:22:44.530 --> 00:22:46.780
So what BigQuery
allows you to do

00:22:46.780 --> 00:22:51.600
is to go from 0 to 3,000
for only 16 seconds--

00:22:51.600 --> 00:22:54.070
20 seconds-- and you
get the equivalent

00:22:54.070 --> 00:22:56.260
of only paying for
what you consume.

00:22:56.260 --> 00:22:58.120
Now, the asterisk
here is that BigQuery

00:22:58.120 --> 00:23:00.280
charges not per second.

00:23:00.280 --> 00:23:02.800
It charges for the
amount of data processed.

00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:04.330
But that's a great
proxy to think

00:23:04.330 --> 00:23:06.977
about when you're
trying to relate this

00:23:06.977 --> 00:23:07.810
to virtual machines.

00:23:10.350 --> 00:23:12.340
But that's all fine and dandy.

00:23:12.340 --> 00:23:14.350
The really key
benefit of serverless

00:23:14.350 --> 00:23:16.360
is that you get a
whole lot of power

00:23:16.360 --> 00:23:18.440
for very short periods of time.

00:23:18.440 --> 00:23:20.500
And you don't even care
that that's happening.

00:23:20.500 --> 00:23:22.270
All you care about
is you have data,

00:23:22.270 --> 00:23:23.920
you wrote a
interesting SQL query,

00:23:23.920 --> 00:23:26.080
and you push the big
red button, and you

00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:27.700
want to get the results.

00:23:27.700 --> 00:23:29.470
That's what you care about.

00:23:29.470 --> 00:23:32.521
Like, sometimes you might
want to geek out and think

00:23:32.521 --> 00:23:33.020
about that.

00:23:33.020 --> 00:23:34.840
But ultimately, this
is where you live.

00:23:34.840 --> 00:23:37.990
And that's the benefit
of serverless, really.

00:23:37.990 --> 00:23:42.400
So that's a lot of power,
very short bits of time.

00:23:42.400 --> 00:23:44.170
And of course, if you
want to extend that

00:23:44.170 --> 00:23:48.760
to TensorFlow and machine
learning technologies,

00:23:48.760 --> 00:23:52.120
our fully managed cloud
machine learning engine service

00:23:52.120 --> 00:23:56.440
allows you to do the exact same
things on top of TPUs and GPUs.

00:23:56.440 --> 00:23:57.640
So you can rent--

00:23:57.640 --> 00:24:00.790
ultimately, you can rent
lots and lots of resources

00:24:00.790 --> 00:24:02.830
for short periods of
time without having

00:24:02.830 --> 00:24:04.970
to worry to stand
up those resources,

00:24:04.970 --> 00:24:06.820
manage those resources
in order for you

00:24:06.820 --> 00:24:08.542
to train your TensorFlow models.

00:24:08.542 --> 00:24:10.000
And once the model
is complete, you

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:11.890
can take that into
your own environment.

00:24:11.890 --> 00:24:14.080
You can take that into
Google Cloud platform

00:24:14.080 --> 00:24:19.150
and deploy it, or even on your
mobile devices, for example.

00:24:19.150 --> 00:24:23.650
And you might have seen
this already in other talks.

00:24:23.650 --> 00:24:27.070
But the one exception to
that whole Jenga analogy

00:24:27.070 --> 00:24:29.729
that I've used
earlier is that there

00:24:29.729 --> 00:24:31.270
has been a whole
lot of research done

00:24:31.270 --> 00:24:32.770
by Google and by
other technologies

00:24:32.770 --> 00:24:35.410
to train models on
large bits of data.

00:24:35.410 --> 00:24:37.670
Google has a good
amount of data, right?

00:24:37.670 --> 00:24:42.430
So we've taken our
investments in technologies,

00:24:42.430 --> 00:24:46.140
and we've externalized them
through simple APIs, rest APIs.

00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:48.640
So any developer can use
them, implement them inside

00:24:48.640 --> 00:24:50.840
of your application.

00:24:50.840 --> 00:24:53.110
Now you have an
AI-driven application.

00:24:53.110 --> 00:24:55.525
And you can certainly get
that next round of funding,

00:24:55.525 --> 00:24:58.360
if you say AI in your name.

00:24:58.360 --> 00:25:01.410
So the third aspect I want
to talk about is real time.

00:25:01.410 --> 00:25:04.462
Real time analytics is
also a solved problem.

00:25:04.462 --> 00:25:05.920
There are certainly
use cases where

00:25:05.920 --> 00:25:08.740
you can have batched
nightly analytics hourly.

00:25:08.740 --> 00:25:13.150
But you have a very easy path
to getting analytics on data

00:25:13.150 --> 00:25:15.740
as it occurs.

00:25:15.740 --> 00:25:19.600
And this is a very typical path
that our organizations take.

00:25:19.600 --> 00:25:23.200
Now, Spotify has blogged
about this quite extensively,

00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:25.580
and other companies, of course.

00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:28.990
"New York Times" is an avid
user of this right here.

00:25:28.990 --> 00:25:31.750
It's a very high
levels of abstraction

00:25:31.750 --> 00:25:35.230
across three services that
allow you to move data

00:25:35.230 --> 00:25:38.680
from the moment it occurs in
your application, to the moment

00:25:38.680 --> 00:25:41.110
that you have
precomputed analytics.

00:25:41.110 --> 00:25:44.650
You have a dashboard you're
trying to develop here.

00:25:44.650 --> 00:25:47.090
And to the moment that you
want to have real-time access

00:25:47.090 --> 00:25:51.360
to SQL, to your analyst, your
data scientist who has data--

00:25:51.360 --> 00:25:54.430
all of that is built. You
just have to implement it.

00:25:54.430 --> 00:25:59.789
And it's a very popular
way of doing things today.

00:25:59.789 --> 00:26:01.580
The last thing I want
to talk about is ETL.

00:26:04.840 --> 00:26:07.090
I have to do ETL sometimes,
and I don't really

00:26:07.090 --> 00:26:09.370
find that necessarily
too enjoyable.

00:26:09.370 --> 00:26:10.340
It's a means to an end.

00:26:10.340 --> 00:26:11.620
There is no glory in it.

00:26:11.620 --> 00:26:14.350
So ETL should be easy.

00:26:14.350 --> 00:26:16.840
We should just get that
in as quickly as possible,

00:26:16.840 --> 00:26:21.520
have it as clean as possible,
and focus on value again.

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:26.390
So I'm going to give you a quick
demo of what I'm talking about.

00:26:26.390 --> 00:26:30.220
So I have this dataset here
called [? Citibank ?] Data.

00:26:33.540 --> 00:26:35.290
It's just a bunch of rows.

00:26:35.290 --> 00:26:37.420
I actually took BigQuery's
public data sets,

00:26:37.420 --> 00:26:39.170
and I exported
them for this demo.

00:26:39.170 --> 00:26:41.130
So just a bunch of information.

00:26:41.130 --> 00:26:41.920
And I ran this--

00:26:41.920 --> 00:26:44.140
I'm going to run
this BigQuery job.

00:26:44.140 --> 00:26:47.920
It's going to take this--
oh, I need to update my CLI.

00:26:47.920 --> 00:26:51.317
I need to-- basically,
I'm taking that data--

00:26:51.317 --> 00:26:52.900
and hopefully, the
Internet holds up--

00:26:52.900 --> 00:26:58.770
and I'm going to load it into
BigQuery for a quick analysis.

00:26:58.770 --> 00:26:59.950
Wait a minute.

00:26:59.950 --> 00:27:01.110
We've got an error.

00:27:04.990 --> 00:27:06.891
Oh, no.

00:27:06.891 --> 00:27:08.190
No, I'm kidding.

00:27:08.190 --> 00:27:10.030
I made that--

00:27:10.030 --> 00:27:14.870
So it looks like the error
says, at position 751,

00:27:14.870 --> 00:27:17.560
there is closed
double quotes that

00:27:17.560 --> 00:27:18.810
have something inside of them.

00:27:18.810 --> 00:27:20.351
All right, well,
I'm going to go to--

00:27:23.170 --> 00:27:24.130
let's go to that line.

00:27:24.130 --> 00:27:26.570
This is the line that
it's talking about.

00:27:26.570 --> 00:27:28.440
Hmm.

00:27:28.440 --> 00:27:30.422
Oh, I see what it is.

00:27:30.422 --> 00:27:32.380
So I have this quotation
here, and inside of it

00:27:32.380 --> 00:27:35.920
is an escape character
that BigQuery

00:27:35.920 --> 00:27:38.560
doesn't like for some reason.

00:27:38.560 --> 00:27:41.410
And of course, there
is an RFC on it

00:27:41.410 --> 00:27:45.070
that says, for comma
delimited values,

00:27:45.070 --> 00:27:48.270
you can only escape
quotes with other quotes.

00:27:48.270 --> 00:27:50.320
And BigQuery is very pedantic.

00:27:50.320 --> 00:27:51.580
It likes RFCs.

00:27:51.580 --> 00:27:53.420
So it rejects your data.

00:27:53.420 --> 00:27:54.576
So what do we do?

00:27:54.576 --> 00:27:56.450
Well, we have this other
tool called Dataprep

00:27:56.450 --> 00:27:58.390
that you can leverage today.

00:27:58.390 --> 00:28:04.810
And I'm going to create a
job inside of Dataflow that

00:28:04.810 --> 00:28:07.049
will hopefully recognize
that information.

00:28:07.049 --> 00:28:09.590
So I'm going to go ahead and go
through the flow of importing

00:28:09.590 --> 00:28:12.370
that file inside of Dataprep.

00:28:12.370 --> 00:28:14.210
There it is.

00:28:14.210 --> 00:28:19.420
It's going to load that file
in, or at least some part of it,

00:28:19.420 --> 00:28:22.090
and then it's going to run
some statistical analysis

00:28:22.090 --> 00:28:24.040
to understand what
exactly is going on.

00:28:24.040 --> 00:28:28.680
It's going to try to understand
what the data looks like--

00:28:28.680 --> 00:28:29.620
did I do that?

00:28:29.620 --> 00:28:30.560
Yep.

00:28:30.560 --> 00:28:32.950
It's going to try to understand
what the data looks like,

00:28:32.950 --> 00:28:34.840
if there are outliers,
if there are errors,

00:28:34.840 --> 00:28:37.090
if it's going to try to
parse this information,

00:28:37.090 --> 00:28:38.510
and so on and so forth.

00:28:38.510 --> 00:28:41.650
So it looks like it created
this thing for me here.

00:28:41.650 --> 00:28:45.310
And this is basically
an ETL job, right?

00:28:45.310 --> 00:28:48.310
I have the file, and it's
going to create a job.

00:28:48.310 --> 00:28:50.670
And this is the recipe
that it's going to execute,

00:28:50.670 --> 00:28:53.130
which is very simple.

00:28:53.130 --> 00:28:57.034
You can certainly make
that more complex.

00:28:57.034 --> 00:28:58.450
So we're going to
look at the data

00:28:58.450 --> 00:29:01.490
and see exactly
what happens here.

00:29:01.490 --> 00:29:03.550
Now we wait.

00:29:03.550 --> 00:29:06.035
But the idea here,
again, is that the--

00:29:08.560 --> 00:29:10.240
wow, it's taking really slow.

00:29:10.240 --> 00:29:13.720
I did not have enough content
prepared to fill this time.

00:29:13.720 --> 00:29:16.100
But ultimately, what
is going to happen

00:29:16.100 --> 00:29:20.159
is it's going to take that
bad row that I threw in there,

00:29:20.159 --> 00:29:21.700
and it's going to
recognize that row.

00:29:21.700 --> 00:29:24.850
And it's going to say,
hey, that is actually

00:29:24.850 --> 00:29:26.620
an escape character.

00:29:26.620 --> 00:29:29.080
It's not conforming to an RFC.

00:29:29.080 --> 00:29:31.970
But we definitely think
it's an escape character.

00:29:31.970 --> 00:29:36.940
So-- wow-- live demos
don't always work, I guess.

00:29:36.940 --> 00:29:39.370
So let's forget about that.

00:29:39.370 --> 00:29:42.650
You're just going to have
to take my word for it.

00:29:42.650 --> 00:29:50.050
So with a tool like Dataprep,
you have one click ETL.

00:29:50.050 --> 00:29:53.840
You can take ugly data
that's, you know--

00:29:53.840 --> 00:29:55.090
if it works.

00:29:55.090 --> 00:29:57.550
You can take ugly data
that doesn't conform

00:29:57.550 --> 00:29:59.462
to any standards,
and you want to-- you

00:29:59.462 --> 00:30:01.712
don't want to write Python
code or anything like that.

00:30:01.712 --> 00:30:04.360
You just want it in one
place for you to analyze.

00:30:04.360 --> 00:30:06.730
So Dataprep is a
fantastic tool for that.

00:30:06.730 --> 00:30:09.370
Under the hood,
Dataprep actually

00:30:09.370 --> 00:30:12.120
creates Apache Beam pipelines.

00:30:12.120 --> 00:30:16.900
It creates these recipes that
turn to Apache Beam pipelines,

00:30:16.900 --> 00:30:19.390
and then executes them on
top of Google Cloud Dataflow.

00:30:19.390 --> 00:30:20.820
So you are familiar
with that technology.

00:30:20.820 --> 00:30:22.970
This is actually a pretty
cool way of doing things.

00:30:22.970 --> 00:30:26.450
So Dataprep is awesome.

00:30:26.450 --> 00:30:32.650
So to very quickly
summarize, the practice

00:30:32.650 --> 00:30:35.110
of data architecture
or data infrastructure

00:30:35.110 --> 00:30:37.220
is moving very, very quickly.

00:30:37.220 --> 00:30:39.700
But there are
pillars that emerge

00:30:39.700 --> 00:30:43.670
that become very obvious
wins very, very quickly.

00:30:43.670 --> 00:30:47.440
And these pillars can
simplify your life.

00:30:47.440 --> 00:30:52.180
And this is kind of like, can
I have things simpler, cheaper,

00:30:52.180 --> 00:30:53.440
and faster?

00:30:53.440 --> 00:30:54.380
Yes.

00:30:54.380 --> 00:30:57.040
And this is because the
architectures fundamentally

00:30:57.040 --> 00:30:57.970
change.

00:30:57.970 --> 00:31:00.820
You can save money and get more.

00:31:00.820 --> 00:31:02.120
That's the lesson here.

00:31:02.120 --> 00:31:04.370
So that's really the four
things that I've identified.

00:31:04.370 --> 00:31:06.280
I'm sure you can
find many, many more.

00:31:06.280 --> 00:31:09.510
I'm going to close this
on saying that, you know,

00:31:09.510 --> 00:31:11.740
the whole concept
of serverless--

00:31:11.740 --> 00:31:14.800
the idea here, again,
is that we want

00:31:14.800 --> 00:31:17.230
to provide the highest level
of abstraction possible.

00:31:17.230 --> 00:31:19.780
We want to automate all the
noise, all the tinkering,

00:31:19.780 --> 00:31:22.750
all the things that are not
really that important to you.

00:31:22.750 --> 00:31:25.390
You don't want to tinker.

00:31:25.390 --> 00:31:28.210
Abstract that away,
automate of that away,

00:31:28.210 --> 00:31:33.100
and give you a surface that
allows you to create value.

00:31:33.100 --> 00:31:35.560
So instead of doing very
little value and a whole lot

00:31:35.560 --> 00:31:37.894
of tinkering, we want to flip
that equation on its head.

00:31:37.894 --> 00:31:39.310
So we want to give
you the ability

00:31:39.310 --> 00:31:41.610
to create a lot of value
in very little tinkering.

00:31:41.610 --> 00:31:43.940
And that's what
serverless means.

00:31:43.940 --> 00:31:44.990
So I thank you very much.

00:31:44.990 --> 00:31:48.550
But before we finish, I'm
going to have Geoffrey

00:31:48.550 --> 00:31:50.110
and Constantijn come on stage.

00:31:50.110 --> 00:31:52.780
We're going to have just
a quick little fireside

00:31:52.780 --> 00:31:55.040
chat where we can take
questions from each other

