the quality of the content.

00:36:38.340 --> 00:36:41.570
So we have a lot of content that
must help me, but what is the--

00:36:41.570 --> 00:36:44.670
I want to quantify the
quality of the content.

00:36:44.670 --> 00:36:47.390
So I use also the video APIs.

00:36:47.390 --> 00:36:50.030
But we also install
TensorFlow and Keras

00:36:50.030 --> 00:36:53.600
to have a better understanding
of what's happening on screen.

00:36:53.600 --> 00:36:55.400
And that generates
a lot of metadata,

00:36:55.400 --> 00:36:58.040
tags, labels that helps me
also in my recommendation.

00:36:58.040 --> 00:36:59.760
So I can prove
that a little more.

00:36:59.760 --> 00:37:01.760
I need to have a
better understanding

00:37:01.760 --> 00:37:03.812
of the quality of content.

00:37:03.812 --> 00:37:05.270
TINO TERESHKO: We
haven't forgotten

00:37:05.270 --> 00:37:06.240
about you, Constantijn.

00:37:06.240 --> 00:37:07.781
So Constantijn was
one of the job men

00:37:07.781 --> 00:37:10.767
that did a whole lot of the
heavy lifting on this project.

00:37:10.767 --> 00:37:12.600
Can you tell us more
about what you've done?

00:37:12.600 --> 00:37:13.725
CONSTANTIJN VISINESCU: Yep.

00:37:13.725 --> 00:37:16.110
When I started with the
project, they already

00:37:16.110 --> 00:37:17.540
were using Hadoop and Spark.

00:37:17.540 --> 00:37:19.580
But they had their
own on-prem cluster

00:37:19.580 --> 00:37:21.570
with a fixed amount of machines.

00:37:21.570 --> 00:37:22.951
So that was also--

00:37:22.951 --> 00:37:24.450
for starters, it
was very expensive,

00:37:24.450 --> 00:37:26.450
because it was always on 24/7.

00:37:26.450 --> 00:37:28.620
And that meant it was--

00:37:28.620 --> 00:37:32.280
either it was not big enough,
because they wanted their data

00:37:32.280 --> 00:37:36.540
now, or it was standing around
costing money doing nothing.

00:37:36.540 --> 00:37:38.860
And it also meant
there was one cluster.

00:37:38.860 --> 00:37:40.680
So if two people
at the same time

00:37:40.680 --> 00:37:42.390
wanted to do something
with the data,

00:37:42.390 --> 00:37:44.440
they had to wait for each other.

00:37:44.440 --> 00:37:47.460
And, yeah, there were multiple
data scientists, multiple data

00:37:47.460 --> 00:37:51.220
engineers, and also,
multiple data analysts.

00:37:51.220 --> 00:37:53.640
And, yeah, if one of them
is using the cluster,

00:37:53.640 --> 00:37:58.080
the others are not doing
anything useful for RTL.

00:37:58.080 --> 00:38:00.310
And at the same
time, I also noticed

00:38:00.310 --> 00:38:04.410
that keeping your own Hadoop
and Spark cluster up and running

00:38:04.410 --> 00:38:07.620
can be quite labor intensive.

00:38:07.620 --> 00:38:11.430
And, yeah, RTL would much
rather spend that effort

00:38:11.430 --> 00:38:14.400
doing useful business things,
than running after their Hadoop

00:38:14.400 --> 00:38:15.720
Spark cluster.

00:38:15.720 --> 00:38:17.830
So I suggested we
could move them over

00:38:17.830 --> 00:38:21.470
to Dataproc for starters.

00:38:21.470 --> 00:38:23.850
And yeah-- and then
they could stop

00:38:23.850 --> 00:38:27.180
worrying about maintaining
them, and at the same time,

00:38:27.180 --> 00:38:31.140
like the example showed, if you
want a 100-node cluster right

00:38:31.140 --> 00:38:32.940
now, that's fine.

00:38:32.940 --> 00:38:35.710
And if your job only
takes 10 minutes,

00:38:35.710 --> 00:38:36.899
then you'd throw it away.

00:38:36.899 --> 00:38:38.940
If there's three people
that want to do something

00:38:38.940 --> 00:38:40.815
at the same time, you
spin up three clusters,

00:38:40.815 --> 00:38:43.420
and then you throw
away three clusters.

00:38:43.420 --> 00:38:45.120
So you can just keep working.

00:38:45.120 --> 00:38:47.520
And, like Tino said
also in the slide,

00:38:47.520 --> 00:38:49.856
if you separate the
data from your compute,

00:38:49.856 --> 00:38:51.480
three people want to
work at the same--

00:38:51.480 --> 00:38:55.050
want to work on the same
data set, that's also fine,

00:38:55.050 --> 00:38:57.750
because you're not tied to get
one data set that lives over

00:38:57.750 --> 00:38:59.700
in that one cluster.

00:38:59.700 --> 00:39:01.881
TINO TERESHKO: How long
did your project take?

00:39:01.881 --> 00:39:04.380
CONSTANTIJN VISINESCU: The whole
project was several months.

00:39:04.380 --> 00:39:09.720
But it also included mostly
understanding the data.

00:39:09.720 --> 00:39:12.330
One of the nice things
about using Google Cloud

00:39:12.330 --> 00:39:15.810
was that setting up the
infrastructure basically

00:39:15.810 --> 00:39:18.640
took me 15 minutes, because
I've done it before.

00:39:18.640 --> 00:39:19.960
But i can just click--

00:39:19.960 --> 00:39:23.040
I want a cluster, I
uploaded their data to--

00:39:23.040 --> 00:39:26.610
I uploaded their data to Google
Cloud [? desk ?] set, and one

00:39:26.610 --> 00:39:28.950
of their existing Spark
jobs that they were already

00:39:28.950 --> 00:39:30.570
running on Premise.

00:39:30.570 --> 00:39:34.350
And within 15 minutes, I
had working infrastructure.

00:39:34.350 --> 00:39:38.190
And I could start spending
the rest of the time actually

00:39:38.190 --> 00:39:40.860
analyzing the data and
figuring out what to do with it

00:39:40.860 --> 00:39:44.340
rather than babysitting
the infrastructure.

00:39:44.340 --> 00:39:46.680
TINO TERESHKO: So
going forward, what

00:39:46.680 --> 00:39:48.395
would be your
recommendation, if you

00:39:48.395 --> 00:39:50.520
were going to implement
this type of infrastructure

00:39:50.520 --> 00:39:51.570
in the future?

00:39:51.570 --> 00:39:54.050
CONSTANTIJN VISINESCU: Well,
if you already have Spark,

00:39:54.050 --> 00:39:57.420
you can just take your existing
Spark jobs that usually

00:39:57.420 --> 00:40:01.770
work on HDFS and put your
data in Google Storage,

00:40:01.770 --> 00:40:04.220
so you get the
date of separation.

00:40:04.220 --> 00:40:07.920
Cloud Dataproc comes with
integration for Google storage,

00:40:07.920 --> 00:40:10.560
basically the same
way you use HDFS.

00:40:10.560 --> 00:40:14.010
So you can just put your
data in Google storage,

00:40:14.010 --> 00:40:18.080
take your existing job,
and change the output

00:40:18.080 --> 00:40:21.870
from HDFS to Google
storage and just run it.

00:40:21.870 --> 00:40:24.665
And that's a great first step.

00:40:24.665 --> 00:40:26.790
TINO TERESHKO: Any other
comments either one of you

00:40:26.790 --> 00:40:28.695
would like to share
about the project?

00:40:28.695 --> 00:40:31.890
GEOFFREY VAN MEER: In a
[? SQL ?] sense, it's easy--

00:40:31.890 --> 00:40:35.650
relatively easy-- to manage.

00:40:35.650 --> 00:40:40.450
So you can allocate your time
more on business problems.

00:40:40.450 --> 00:40:42.390
So the maintenance
is relatively easy.

00:40:42.390 --> 00:40:44.140
It's very transparent.

00:40:44.140 --> 00:40:46.530
And what I also
like is that each--

00:40:46.530 --> 00:40:48.510
as you explained,
data scientists come

00:40:48.510 --> 00:40:51.240
to the office in the morning,
they spin up at the cluster,

00:40:51.240 --> 00:40:53.160
and here they go.

00:40:53.160 --> 00:40:56.430
So they're not interfering
with each other.

00:40:56.430 --> 00:40:59.490
And they use all the
same-- the old cluster,

00:40:59.490 --> 00:41:02.590
all the applications on there,
so they can play around.

00:41:02.590 --> 00:41:06.900
And today, data starts, I
want a cluster of 10 machines.

00:41:06.900 --> 00:41:09.570
And tomorrow, I'll do a
different type of algorithm,

00:41:09.570 --> 00:41:13.770
and I'll spin up a cluster
of 50 machines after them.

00:41:13.770 --> 00:41:16.080
And I know exactly, as a
manager, who was using what.

00:41:16.080 --> 00:41:20.820
So I can also say, you
burned too much, lower down,

00:41:20.820 --> 00:41:21.879
or spin up.

00:41:21.879 --> 00:41:22.920
TINO TERESHKO: Fantastic.

00:41:22.920 --> 00:41:24.269
Constantijn?

00:41:24.269 --> 00:41:25.810
CONSTANTIJN VISINESCU:
Yeah, that's--

00:41:25.810 --> 00:41:29.370
like Geoffrey said, that really
helped their data scientists.

00:41:29.370 --> 00:41:32.160
And it also helped
later on the project,

00:41:32.160 --> 00:41:33.830
because the original
project used

00:41:33.830 --> 00:41:37.730
Spark to put their
data into an existing--

00:41:37.730 --> 00:41:41.400
pre-process their data
into a normal SQL database.

00:41:41.400 --> 00:41:44.100
But that means that
sometimes that, when

00:41:44.100 --> 00:41:47.570
some of the data engineers are
busy, I literally told them--

00:41:47.570 --> 00:41:52.260
heard them tell one of
the data analysts, like,

00:41:52.260 --> 00:41:55.710
can you please not do your work
until 2:00 in the afternoon,

00:41:55.710 --> 00:41:58.700
because I'm busy
using the database?

00:41:58.700 --> 00:42:02.490
And, yeah, like, I understand
from a technical's perspective,

00:42:02.490 --> 00:42:04.860
because, the data
engineer was just

00:42:04.860 --> 00:42:06.660
keeping the database very busy.

00:42:06.660 --> 00:42:08.100
But you don't want that.

00:42:08.100 --> 00:42:11.440
So instead of putting the
data into a normal database,

00:42:11.440 --> 00:42:14.400
we put the data in
BigQuery, as you saw.

00:42:14.400 --> 00:42:17.190
And RTL has lots
of data because,

00:42:17.190 --> 00:42:20.460
like Geoffrey said,
there's a large multimedia

00:42:20.460 --> 00:42:21.960
presence on the
Internet from RTL,

00:42:21.960 --> 00:42:24.990
not just the RTL label,
but multiple labels.

00:42:24.990 --> 00:42:27.910
And that literally generates
billions of data points.

00:42:27.910 --> 00:42:30.790
And I can just put
those into BigQuery.

00:42:30.790 --> 00:42:32.750
I don't even have to
aggregate them anymore.

00:42:32.750 --> 00:42:35.240
So before, all the
data was aggregated,

00:42:35.240 --> 00:42:39.040
and you could only get the
reports that the aggregation

00:42:39.040 --> 00:42:40.990
was specifically made for.

00:42:40.990 --> 00:42:43.030
And now all the data
is just in there.

00:42:43.030 --> 00:42:47.980
And yeah, there are some
premade aggregations

00:42:47.980 --> 00:42:49.500
to make things
easier on the users.

00:42:49.500 --> 00:42:51.530
But it's basically
do whatever you want.

00:42:51.530 --> 00:42:53.800
And again, because
it's serverless,

00:42:53.800 --> 00:42:56.380
as long as you don't request
more compute power than Google

00:42:56.380 --> 00:43:00.172
has, like, you don't make Google
crash, then they can go nuts.

00:43:00.172 --> 00:43:01.540
TINO TERESHKO: Fantastic.

00:43:01.540 --> 00:43:02.910
[LAUGHTER]

00:43:02.910 --> 00:43:04.920
And we challenge you to do that.

00:43:04.920 --> 00:43:06.089
[LAUGHTER]

00:43:06.089 --> 00:43:08.630
So we have a few minutes to take
questions from the audience.

00:43:08.630 --> 00:43:10.490
So please raise your
hand, and there will

00:43:10.490 --> 00:43:12.689
be a microphone coming around.

00:43:12.689 --> 00:43:13.480
Please, right here.

00:43:16.167 --> 00:43:17.000
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:43:17.000 --> 00:43:20.664
What's the difference
between BigQuery and Spanner?

00:43:20.664 --> 00:43:22.080
TINO TERESHKO:
Fantastic question.

00:43:22.080 --> 00:43:25.830
So BigQuery and Spanner are
used extensively internally

00:43:25.830 --> 00:43:27.960
at Google and by our clients.

00:43:27.960 --> 00:43:30.150
The easiest way to think
about it is BigQuery

00:43:30.150 --> 00:43:33.060
is great for
analytics, whereas, you

00:43:33.060 --> 00:43:35.490
know, things like aggregating
lots of information.

00:43:35.490 --> 00:43:38.835
And Spanner is a fantastic
tool to create an application

00:43:38.835 --> 00:43:41.900
or to have an
operational database.

00:43:41.900 --> 00:43:44.274
They go hand in hand together.

00:43:44.274 --> 00:43:45.496
Yeah.

00:43:45.496 --> 00:43:47.995
And they actually both speak
the same standard SQL language.

00:43:50.850 --> 00:43:51.890
Any other questions?

00:43:51.890 --> 00:43:52.060
Please.

00:43:52.060 --> 00:43:52.840
CONSTANTIJN VISINESCU: You have
another question over there.

00:43:52.840 --> 00:43:53.917
TINO TERESHKO: Yeah.

00:43:53.917 --> 00:43:56.840
GEOFFREY VAN MEER: Over there.

00:43:56.840 --> 00:44:00.330
AUDIENCE: Your 20 second
query of what was it?

00:44:00.330 --> 00:44:01.970
3,000 nodes?

00:44:01.970 --> 00:44:03.395
What's the money being spent?

00:44:03.395 --> 00:44:05.020
TINO TERESHKO: That's
a great question.

00:44:05.020 --> 00:44:09.600
So that particular query
cost about $10 to run.

00:44:09.600 --> 00:44:12.090
So the query has two pricing
models, specifically.

00:44:12.090 --> 00:44:14.490
One is paper job, as I did.

00:44:14.490 --> 00:44:18.350
And for folks who are
at a larger scale, who

00:44:18.350 --> 00:44:21.690
have specific use cases, you
can pay a flat monthly fee

00:44:21.690 --> 00:44:23.018
that includes all your queries.

00:44:23.018 --> 00:44:23.994
Good question.

00:44:33.270 --> 00:44:35.010
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:44:35.010 --> 00:44:38.400
Like a lot of BI
departments, in our company,

00:44:38.400 --> 00:44:41.910
we're a bit of a bottleneck when
it comes to data preparation.

00:44:41.910 --> 00:44:45.540
So Dataprep looks cool, because
we can put that out there.

00:44:45.540 --> 00:44:48.138
How difficult would
it be to productionize

00:44:48.138 --> 00:44:51.607
the output of Dataprep?

00:44:51.607 --> 00:44:53.190
TINO TERESHKO: That's
a good question.

00:44:53.190 --> 00:44:55.920
In terms of productionize, you
want to put this into a place

00:44:55.920 --> 00:44:57.930
where analysts can work on it.

00:44:57.930 --> 00:45:00.860
AUDIENCE: Sure, selected
analysts might get 20 files.

00:45:00.860 --> 00:45:01.910
They do their thing.

00:45:01.910 --> 00:45:04.020
And then we've got
6,000 files that we

00:45:04.020 --> 00:45:06.090
need to run through
a nightly process.

00:45:06.090 --> 00:45:07.799
And so that kind of
production, I think--

00:45:07.799 --> 00:45:10.214
TINO TERESHKO: Yeah, that is
an intrinsic-- that's exactly

00:45:10.214 --> 00:45:11.340
the use case for Dataprep.

00:45:11.340 --> 00:45:13.110
So it's an intrinsic
quality of that service.

00:45:13.110 --> 00:45:13.515
CONSTANTIJN VISINESCU:
Actually, we

00:45:13.515 --> 00:45:16.380
had to do pretty much the
exact same thing at RTL.

00:45:16.380 --> 00:45:18.180
One of the nice
things about BigQuery

00:45:18.180 --> 00:45:19.800
is that, next to
its native custom

00:45:19.800 --> 00:45:21.390
drivers that are
really fast, it also

00:45:21.390 --> 00:45:24.570
has an existing ODBC driver.

00:45:24.570 --> 00:45:28.410
And it speaks standard ANSI SQL.

00:45:28.410 --> 00:45:32.490
So it can talk with anything
that can talk SQL over ODBC.

00:45:32.490 --> 00:45:36.082
And that is basically--
anything that calls itself

00:45:36.082 --> 00:45:38.910
a data analytics tool
can talk with it.

00:45:38.910 --> 00:45:43.850
So at RTL, the main tool
for business intelligence

00:45:43.850 --> 00:45:46.740
is Business Objects.

00:45:46.740 --> 00:45:49.770
And I just set up an ODBC query.

00:45:49.770 --> 00:45:53.400
And within a couple
days, Business Objects

00:45:53.400 --> 00:45:55.380
could talk the
BigQuery and no one

00:45:55.380 --> 00:45:57.010
would notice the difference.

00:45:57.010 --> 00:45:58.800
And there's also
several other tools

00:45:58.800 --> 00:46:01.080
with RTL, just their
own departments using

00:46:01.080 --> 00:46:02.340
their own tools.

00:46:02.340 --> 00:46:06.070
And as long as they can talk
ODBC with standard ANSI SQL,

00:46:06.070 --> 00:46:07.905
then it just works.

00:46:07.905 --> 00:46:09.780
TINO TERESHKO: We're
essentially out of time,

00:46:09.780 --> 00:46:12.240
but we'll take one final
question, right there.

00:46:12.240 --> 00:46:13.240
Thank you.

00:46:13.240 --> 00:46:15.820
AUDIENCE: Thanks for
taking my question.

00:46:15.820 --> 00:46:20.700
So, a lot of analytic projects
live in an IT landscape, not

00:46:20.700 --> 00:46:22.420
just a Google landscape.

00:46:22.420 --> 00:46:28.140
Can you say something about
how to roll out into production

00:46:28.140 --> 00:46:29.990
both code and infrastructure?

00:46:29.990 --> 00:46:34.830
And how do you see integration
with existing, let's say,

00:46:34.830 --> 00:46:36.645
applications and
deployment pipeline?

00:46:36.645 --> 00:46:40.120
So I'm thinking, so how do
you use this stuff with Git?

00:46:40.120 --> 00:46:42.660
How do you roll all that stuff?

00:46:42.660 --> 00:46:43.562
TINO TERESHKO: Yeah.

00:46:43.562 --> 00:46:45.570
CONSTANTIJN VISINESCU:
At RTL, we had--

00:46:45.570 --> 00:46:46.990
our pipelines were in code.

00:46:46.990 --> 00:46:48.960
There's two out there
called the Air Flow

00:46:48.960 --> 00:46:51.210
and that's also from Apache.

00:46:51.210 --> 00:46:55.420
And that allows you to model
all your data pipelines as code.

00:46:55.420 --> 00:46:58.140
So there's jobs in there
like, spin up a cluster,

00:46:58.140 --> 00:47:00.810
run these Spark jobs,
spin down the cluster,

00:47:00.810 --> 00:47:02.450
and take the output
from that Spark job

00:47:02.450 --> 00:47:03.900
and put it into BigQuery.

00:47:03.900 --> 00:47:05.960
That's all code.

00:47:05.960 --> 00:47:08.750
And that code lives
in a Git repository.

00:47:08.750 --> 00:47:11.910
And there's a Jenkins on
top of that Git repository.

00:47:11.910 --> 00:47:15.580
So if I commit to master,
Jenkins runs the tests.

00:47:15.580 --> 00:47:17.850
If the tests look
good, it automatically

00:47:17.850 --> 00:47:19.700
puts it to the
accept environment.

00:47:19.700 --> 00:47:24.769
And if I hit the button, the
same code goes to production.

00:47:24.769 --> 00:47:26.310
TINO TERESHKO: The
value of ecosystem

00:47:26.310 --> 00:47:30.900
that creates orchestration
and monitoring, things

00:47:30.900 --> 00:47:32.550
like that can't be understated.

00:47:32.550 --> 00:47:35.000
So Google specifically
invested in partnerships

00:47:35.000 --> 00:47:39.210
and in supporting open source
technologies like Airflow

00:47:39.210 --> 00:47:41.210
that we've extended
into our services.

00:47:41.210 --> 00:47:42.884
And if there are
technologies that

00:47:42.884 --> 00:47:44.550
are compelling that
we don't know about,

00:47:44.550 --> 00:47:47.430
we want to know
about them as well.

00:47:47.430 --> 00:47:48.660
Thank you, folks.

00:47:48.660 --> 00:47:51.210
I think this is-- we
have run out of time.

00:47:51.210 --> 00:47:52.520
But thanks for your time.

00:47:52.520 --> 00:47:53.020
Great.

00:47:53.020 --> 00:47:54.220
And thank you as well.

00:47:54.220 --> 00:47:56.070
[APPLAUSE]

